WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.785
Before deserializing data, identify the source.

2
00:00:02.785 --> 00:00:18.702
User-submitted data, uploaded files, API request bodies, webhooks, queue
messages, cache entries, database fields, configuration files, partner
feeds, third-party API responses, generated machine output, and internal
service messages all deserve different trust decisions.

3
00:00:18.702 --> 00:00:22.681
The format may be the same, but the surrounding control and risk are not.

4
00:00:22.681 --> 00:00:26.925
Trusted configuration and untrusted content should be separated clearly.

5
00:00:26.925 --> 00:00:31.700
A reviewed deployment configuration file may be allowed to change runtime
behavior.

6
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:37.271
A user-uploaded file or partner feed should usually be treated as data
that must fit a narrow schema.

7
00:00:37.271 --> 00:00:47.882
A queue message from another internal service may be more trusted than
public input, but it can still be malformed, stale, replayed, or produced
by a compromised or misconfigured component.

8
00:00:47.882 --> 00:00:53.452
Source, owner, freshness, sensitivity, and intended use should be known
before deserialization.

9
00:00:53.452 --> 00:00:55.773
Source tells you where the data came from.

10
00:00:55.773 --> 00:00:57.630
Owner tells you who can change it.

11
00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:00.747
Freshness tells you whether it may be stale or replayed.

12
00:01:00.747 --> 00:01:04.527
Sensitivity tells you how carefully it should be stored and logged.

13
00:01:04.527 --> 00:01:08.308
Intended use tells you what the application is allowed to do with it.

14
00:01:08.308 --> 00:01:13.348
These questions guide parser settings, validation rules, storage, and
access controls.

15
00:01:13.348 --> 00:01:16.266
A durable rule is to trust by design, not by accident.

16
00:01:16.266 --> 00:01:25.152
If code treats every cache entry, database field, service message, and
configuration blob as safe merely because it is internal, mistakes become
hard to find.

17
00:01:25.152 --> 00:01:27.672
Clear boundaries make secure review easier.

18
00:01:27.672 --> 00:01:36.360
Reviewers can see which data is untrusted, which data is trusted
configuration, and which data needs validation before it influences
important behavior.
